install git, jenkins, docker (and give permissions), terraform, kubectl
clone the repo -> https://github.com/sattiPavan1999/simple-microservices-starter.git
go to terraform and then give init, plan, apply commands to create VPC, EKS, and etc ..

write pipeline and store images in dockerhub
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('cleanws') {
            steps {
                cleanWs()
            }
        }
        stage('code') {
            steps {
                git branch: 'main', url: 'https://github.com/sattiPavan1999/simple-microservices-starter.git'
            }
        }
        stage('build image') {
            steps {
                sh "docker build -t pavanreddy1999/node-project-frontend:v1 frontend/."
                sh "docker build -t pavanreddy1999/node-project-users:v1 users-service/."
                sh "docker build -t pavanreddy1999/node-project-products:v1 products-service/."
            }
        }
        stage('image push') {
            steps {
                script {
                    withDockerRegistry(credentialsId: 'dockerhub-id') {
                        sh "docker push pavanreddy1999/node-project-frontend:v1"
                        sh "docker push pavanreddy1999/node-project-users:v1"
                        sh "docker push pavanreddy1999/node-project-products:v1"
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}


then apply k8s commands 


2. Create the namespace and DB secrets
* * *
kubectl apply -f 00-namespace.yaml

kubectl create secret generic users-db-secret -n microservices \
  --from-literal=DATABASE_URL="$(aws ssm get-parameter --name /simple-microservices-dev/users/DATABASE_URL --with-decryption --query Parameter.Value --output text)"

kubectl create secret generic products-db-secret -n microservices \
  --from-literal=DATABASE_URL="$(aws ssm get-parameter --name /simple-microservices-dev/products/DATABASE_URL --with-decryption --query Parameter.Value --output text


3. Deploy the backends (after replacing DOCKERHUB_USERNAME in the YAMLs and pushing your images)

bash
kubectl apply -f users.yaml -f products.yaml
kubectl get svc -n microservices -w

Wait until both show an EXTERNAL-IP hostname, then press Ctrl+C.

4. Set those hostnames in frontend.yaml, then deploy it

bash
kubectl apply -f frontend.yaml
kubectl get svc frontend -n microservices

Open the frontend hostname in your browser.

